WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.030
Feedback is the foundation of performance improvement.

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:08.657
Without feedback, performers may repeat the same approach and mistake familiarity for effectiveness.

3
00:00:08.657 --> 00:00:12.120
Useful feedback connects actions, decisions, outcomes, and context.

4
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:20.344
It helps the performer understand what worked, what did not, and what needs to change before the next attempt.

5
00:00:20.344 --> 00:00:23.374
Measurement should include more than final outcomes.

6
00:00:23.374 --> 00:00:29.867
Outcome measures matter, but they can hide whether the process was sound or merely lucky.

7
00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:38.092
Process measures, quality measures, decision records, peer review, and after-action reviews help show how the result was produced.

8
00:00:38.092 --> 00:00:47.182
They make it easier to improve the reasoning and behavior behind the outcome, not just celebrate or criticize the end state.

9
00:00:47.182 --> 00:00:51.510
Calibration means matching confidence to actual performance, accuracy, or likelihood.

10
00:00:51.510 --> 00:01:01.033
Poor calibration can show up as overconfidence, where certainty exceeds evidence, or underconfidence, where useful skill is not trusted enough to act.

11
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:09.690
Calibration improves when performers compare expectations with results, review decisions honestly, and learn which cues are reliable under which conditions.

12
00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:15.751
The challenge is to measure what matters without turning performance into shallow score-chasing.

13
00:01:15.751 --> 00:01:18.781
Vanity metrics can create activity without capability.

14
00:01:18.781 --> 00:01:28.736
A strong measurement system asks whether the measure reflects real quality, whether it encourages the desired behavior, and whether it helps people improve.

15
00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:34.796
The purpose of measurement is learning and reliability, not a scoreboard that rewards shortcuts.
